.TH FOPEN 3
.SH NAME
fopen, freopen, fdopen \- open a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

FILE *
fopen(filename, type)
char *filename, *type;

FILE *
freopen(filename, type, stream)
char *filename, *type;
FILE *stream;

FILE *
fdopen(fildes, type)
int fildes;
char *type;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fopen
opens the file named by
.I filename
and returns a pointer to a stream for it.
.I type
is a string, one of:
.TP 8
.B r
open for reading
.TP
.B w
truncate to nothing, or create, for writing
.TP
.B a
append: open for writing at the end of the file, or create it
.TP
.B r+
open for reading and writing, at the start
.TP
.B w+
truncate or create, for reading and writing
.TP
.B a+
open for reading and writing at the end of the file, or create it
.PP
A
.B b
after the first letter, as in rb, w+b or r+b, is accepted and changes
nothing: XENIX makes no difference between text and binary files. A
file that is created has the mode 0666, less the bits set in the
process's
.BR umask (2).
.PP
When a stream is open for both reading and writing, a read may not
follow a write, nor a write a read, without a call to
.B fseek
or
.B rewind
between them.
.PP
.B freopen
closes the file of
.IR stream ,
then opens
.I filename
on the same stream. Its usual use is to give
.BR stdin ,
.B stdout
or
.B stderr
another file:
.PP
.nf
	if (freopen("log", "a", stdout) == NULL)
		perror("log");
.fi
.PP
.B fdopen
makes a stream for a file descriptor that is already open, as from
.BR open (2),
.BR pipe (2)
or
.BR dup (2).
The
.I type
must agree with the way the descriptor was opened.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
The stream, or NULL if the file could not be opened, the
.I type
is not one of these, or 20 streams are already open.
.SH ERRORS
When the file cannot be opened, errno is set by
.BR open (2)
or
.BR creat (2):
for instance ENOENT if it does not exist, or EACCES if permission is
denied.
.SH SEE ALSO
open(2), creat(2), fclose(3), fseek(3), setbuf(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B fopen
function first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX. The
.B fdopen
and
.B freopen
functions first appeared in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH BUGS
With type a, the file is positioned at its end once, when it is opened;
another process writing to the file at the same time may have its
output overwritten.
